                                "example_id": "31ec9707-ef94-453a-a15b-88c483febf48",
                                "sense_id": "16ad2270-1575-36dd-8ddf-f4a1a3378ad0",
                                "text": "1967 April, Donald Keith, The Time Machine Hunts a Treasure, Boys' Life, page 51,\nShe stared at us. “Hardly. You must be from far off, not to know about Quantrill′s raids. Last March the bushwhackers rode into Aubrey, shot every man in town, stole everything, burned down houses. […] ”"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1ccb278d-4cbb-48c5-a5a3-9fc8c69821a0",
                                "sense_id": "16ad2270-1575-36dd-8ddf-f4a1a3378ad0",
                                "text": "Many bushwhackers were young men born to families of southern heritage who were generally wealthier and more likely to own slaves than the typical Missouri farmer.[…]His eldest daughter, Elisa, was an effective bushwhacker spy and informant."
